WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.402
-Our first guest tonight is an

00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:04.437
Emmy-winning comedian and writer
you know her from her work on

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:06.005
"Black-ish" and
"Curb Your Enthusiasm."

00:00:06.005 --> 00:00:09.008
Part one of the second season
of her comedy series

00:00:09.008 --> 00:00:11.077
"The Upshaws" begins
streaming tomorrow on Netflix.

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:12.545
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:15.147
-I heard my name and "clothes."
What'd I get? What'd I get?

00:00:15.147 --> 00:00:17.383
-Something from me.

00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:19.352
-I'm about to be
fake grateful, aren't I?

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:22.422
-Relax, little girl.
Vintage is in.

00:00:22.422 --> 00:00:24.857
-What's The Limited?
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:24.857 --> 00:00:28.060
Honey, your aunt is trying to do
something nice, okay?

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.562
At least try something on.

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:32.064
-Thank you, Aunt Lucretia.

00:00:32.064 --> 00:00:34.567
-Hey, you might find
something that you like.

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:37.270
She ain't gonna want
none of that.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.639
-I don't care.
It gets it out of my house.

00:00:39.639 --> 00:00:41.407
I was gonna drive it down
to the church,

00:00:41.407 --> 00:00:43.809
but your coffee
doesn't require a donation

00:00:43.809 --> 00:00:45.779
to the building fund.

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:47.146
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:47.146 --> 00:00:49.382
our friend Wanda Sykes,
everybody.

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:51.451
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:01:03.163 --> 00:01:05.298
We are so happy
to have you here, Wanda.

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:07.567
-Why, thank you.
Happy to be here.

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:09.068
-How are you feeling about the

00:01:09.068 --> 00:01:10.236
state of the world right now?

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:13.506
-Oh, the world's on fire, Seth.

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:15.374
It's terrible. It's terrible.

00:01:15.374 --> 00:01:16.810
-It's terrible.
-It is.

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:19.912
-I will say, it's very lovely to
have your show, "The Upshaws,"

00:01:19.912 --> 00:01:23.515
which is sort of,
I mean, a throwback, in a way,

00:01:23.515 --> 00:01:26.019
of a kind of family sitcom
I grew up watching,

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:27.853
which is quite lovely
in times like these.

00:01:27.853 --> 00:01:29.622
-Yes, yes. Absolutely.

00:01:29.622 --> 00:01:31.323
You know, that's --

00:01:31.323 --> 00:01:33.325
Like you said,
that's what I grew up watching.

00:01:33.325 --> 00:01:37.230
And when Mike Epps said,
"Hey, I want to do a show about

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:40.933
a working-class black family,"
I was like,

00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:43.069
"Well, that's kind of bland."

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:44.770
And I thought about it.
I was like, "Oh, shoot.

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:46.338
You know what?
They don't exist anymore."

00:01:46.338 --> 00:01:47.473
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:47.473 --> 00:01:52.144
So very excited about it
and work with, you know,

00:01:52.144 --> 00:01:53.379
Regina Hicks on it.

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:56.048
And we put it together,
and it's really funny.

00:01:56.048 --> 00:01:57.183
We just love it, yeah.

00:01:57.183 --> 00:01:58.550
-You get to be
in front of the camera

00:01:58.550 --> 00:01:59.985
and behind the scenes, as well.

00:01:59.985 --> 00:02:02.121
You're Aunt Lucretia,
as we saw there.

00:02:02.121 --> 00:02:04.290
I'm assuming you get to pull
from your own family history

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.392
when you get to write
a colorful character like this.

00:02:06.392 --> 00:02:08.661
-You know, that's the beauty
about this show.

00:02:08.661 --> 00:02:11.063
So many people approach us
and say, "You know what?

00:02:11.063 --> 00:02:14.701
That's my aunt. That's my uncle.
That's my dad."

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:18.404
You know, so it's like you do --
It's very relatable.

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:19.738
You get to see--

00:02:19.738 --> 00:02:21.507
-Does your family get worried
when you're working on

00:02:21.507 --> 00:02:23.709
a family sitcom,
that you're gonna pull

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:26.613
specifically from your history?

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:29.115
-They might be a little nervous.

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:30.950
They might see
a few things pop up

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:32.618
every now and then, you know?

00:02:32.618 --> 00:02:34.954
-But, usually, they're cool.
-Yes, they are.

00:02:34.954 --> 00:02:36.288
They're cool. They're cool.

00:02:36.288 --> 00:02:38.858
You know, I send flowers
and, you know, stuff like that.

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:40.559
-Oh, very...
That's right.

00:02:40.559 --> 00:02:42.061
You've been in the business
a long time.

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:44.797
You know how to handle this.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:47.666
-So, your twins, right?
They're...

00:02:47.666 --> 00:02:48.801
-I have twins, yes.

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:50.803
-Yeah, they just finished
seventh grade?

00:02:50.803 --> 00:02:51.804
-Yes, yes.
-Unbelievable.

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:53.939
That's a real --
They're real adults now.

00:02:53.939 --> 00:02:55.974
-Yeah. Don't tell them that.
-Yeah.

00:02:55.974 --> 00:02:57.109
Where are you gonna --

00:02:57.109 --> 00:02:58.610
What are you gonna do with
them this summer?

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:00.112
-Well, they're in France
right now.

00:03:00.112 --> 00:03:04.951
So, you know, they --
My wife is French.

00:03:04.951 --> 00:03:09.122
She's white, but I say French
because it sounds classier.

00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:11.157
[ Laughter ]

00:03:13.392 --> 00:03:14.793
-That's such a fancy way of
saying -- Yeah, yeah.

00:03:14.793 --> 00:03:16.462
-Yeah, it's so fancy.

00:03:16.462 --> 00:03:18.597
So, yeah, they're over there,
you know,

00:03:18.597 --> 00:03:20.066
with their cousins, and, also,

00:03:20.066 --> 00:03:23.235
they'll spend
most of the summer over there.

00:03:23.235 --> 00:03:26.405
And if, you know,
[bleep] gets rough here,

00:03:26.405 --> 00:03:27.506
they might not come back.

00:03:27.506 --> 00:03:29.641
I don't know. Who knows?

00:03:29.641 --> 00:03:31.944
-We've talked in the past about
how your wife, Alex,

00:03:31.944 --> 00:03:33.779
takes advantage of the fact
that you don't speak French

00:03:33.779 --> 00:03:35.881
and will sometimes communicate
with the kids that way.

00:03:35.881 --> 00:03:38.584
-Mm-hmm.
-Is she continuing to do this?

00:03:38.584 --> 00:03:41.521
-Yes, but I've been --
You know, I've been doing

00:03:41.521 --> 00:03:46.025
the Duolingo thing, so I'm
learning more French, you know,

00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:47.393
so I'm getting good at it.

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:52.331
But my wife is awful when it
comes to, you know, like,

00:03:52.331 --> 00:03:53.632
speaking French with me.

00:03:53.632 --> 00:03:54.967
She's too hard on me, you know?

00:03:54.967 --> 00:03:56.436
-Oh, really?
-Yeah, and it hurts my feelings.

00:03:56.436 --> 00:03:57.803
Like, she'll --
You know, it's like,

00:03:57.803 --> 00:03:59.038
I'm trying to say a word.

00:03:59.038 --> 00:04:00.072
She's like, "No, no.

00:04:00.072 --> 00:04:02.508
That's not how you
say it, baby."

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:04.611
Or they'll laugh at me.

00:04:04.611 --> 00:04:09.249
So I've decided that I am going
to become fluent in French

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:12.852
and never speak a word
of French to her.

00:04:12.852 --> 00:04:14.220
I'm gonna go to France,

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:18.157
I'm gonna parlez-vous my ass
all over the place,

00:04:18.157 --> 00:04:20.759
and she's only getting English.

00:04:20.759 --> 00:04:22.795
-I noticed, now,
were you miming a cigarette

00:04:22.795 --> 00:04:24.063
because she actually smokes

00:04:24.063 --> 00:04:25.798
or is that just how you
mime all French people?

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:27.166
-That's how I mime
all French people.

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:29.835
-Yeah. I do it, too.

00:04:29.835 --> 00:04:31.704
-Even the babies.

00:04:34.072 --> 00:04:36.342
[ French accent ]
Goo-goo, ga-ga.

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:38.010
-Yeah.

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:41.146
[ French accent ] Yeah,
I do not like this stroller.

00:04:41.847 --> 00:04:44.584
You celebrated Pride --
not here in the city,

00:04:44.584 --> 00:04:46.085
but you were on Fire Island.

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:48.153
What's it like --
what's it like out there?

00:04:48.153 --> 00:04:52.391
-Oh, I love my community
over there on Fire Island,

00:04:52.391 --> 00:04:54.027
you know, in the grove.

00:04:54.027 --> 00:04:56.261
And we -- It's --
Okay, I've got to say,

00:04:56.261 --> 00:04:59.766
we might skew a little
on the older side,

00:04:59.766 --> 00:05:03.602
so you might not see,
you know, the parade

00:05:03.602 --> 00:05:05.071
like you do here in New York.

00:05:05.071 --> 00:05:08.474
You know, it's a small island,
so there's a lot of, you know,

00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:12.945
people on the Rascal scooters
and stuff going by.

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:15.648
But that's actually
their mode of transportation.

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:17.417
It's like, you know --
But the parade,

00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:19.485
they just stick a rainbow
on the front of it,

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:22.221
and, you know, we good.

00:05:22.221 --> 00:05:23.823
-But, you know, people who do
a Pride parade

00:05:23.823 --> 00:05:25.858
that are that age, they've
really been through the battles.

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:27.794
-They have been through it.

00:05:27.794 --> 00:05:29.161
-Yeah.
-Yes, absolutely.

00:05:29.161 --> 00:05:31.496
-So there's even something
more to celebrate about somebody

00:05:31.496 --> 00:05:33.465
at the age where they're on
their Rascal, you know,

00:05:33.465 --> 00:05:35.500
even at a scary time
like this, right?

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:36.635
It's important to note

00:05:36.635 --> 00:05:38.137
that we're farther ahead
than we were.

00:05:38.137 --> 00:05:39.171
-Yes, yes.

00:05:39.171 --> 00:05:41.040
But just don't want
to go backwards.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:44.677
And the parade,
actually, and this Pride,

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:48.080
it has more weight to it
because you know we're next.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.515
-Yeah, of course.

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:52.685
-They're coming after us next,
so it has much more meaning now.

00:05:52.685 --> 00:05:54.553
-Well, it's really nice,
and I think there are times

00:05:54.553 --> 00:05:56.555
like that and especially,
you know, in a month like this,

00:05:56.555 --> 00:05:57.857
it's really important to
think about that

00:05:57.857 --> 00:05:59.258
and remember that
it can all be taken away.

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:00.392
-Exactly.
-You know, it's not permanent.

00:06:00.392 --> 00:06:03.496
-Exactly. And you have
no idea how far --

00:06:03.496 --> 00:06:04.831
what else they're
gonna take away.

00:06:04.831 --> 00:06:06.832
I'm like, "Do I need to go
further north?"

00:06:06.832 --> 00:06:08.568
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:06:10.836 --> 00:06:12.404
-You know, in Canada,
there are parts of Canada

00:06:12.404 --> 00:06:13.639
where they speak French, too.

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:15.007
You're going to be ready to go.
-There you go.

00:06:16.909 --> 00:06:18.077
[ French accent ] Bonjour.

00:06:18.077 --> 00:06:20.880
-Oh, you're --
Duolingo is really working.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:22.714
That was perfect.

00:06:22.714 --> 00:06:23.949
Thank you for being in
this room with us.

00:06:23.949 --> 00:06:26.352
While we agree it's nuts,
I really appreciate it.

00:06:26.352 --> 00:06:27.720
-Appreciate it.
-Always a pleasure.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.388
You guys, that's Wanda Sykes.

00:06:29.388 --> 00:06:32.524
Part one of the second season
of "The Upshaws"

00:06:32.524 --> 00:06:33.860
begins streaming tomorrow
on Netflix.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:36.396
We'll be right back with Kesha,
everybody.

